# Digilent Basys 3 (Xilinx XC7A35T-1CPG236C): `iso_display_pad.v` — an
# ISO 7816 card and an SSD1306 panel in one bitstream, on one serial port.
#
# The top is `iso_display_pad`, the thin wrapper that holds the PLL and the
# three bidirectional balls; `iso_display` itself takes `io_i` and drives
# `io_oe`/`io_o` so a testbench can put a model card on the contact.
#
# This file is the union of `iso7816_terminal.rcf` and
# `ssd1306_console.rcf` and adds nothing of its own: the two halves share
# the clock, the serial port, the LEDs and the digits, and their other pins
# do not overlap.
#
# PROVENANCE: ball names transcribed from `examples/soc/board/basys3.rcf`,
# which transcribes Digilent's published `Basys-3-Master.xdc`. Two lines of
# that file have been CHECKED on this board and one was wrong, so treat the
# rest as a reading: A18/B18 are the FPGA's transmit and receive into the
# USB-UART bridge (CHECKED 8 October 2026 by echoing bytes through
# `uart_loop.v`), and `sw[0]`/`led[0]` are at the RIGHT-hand end (CHECKED
# the same day, correcting a comment that said left).
#
# ===================================================================
# SAFETY: WHAT IS ON THE OTHER END OF THESE FOUR BALLS
# ===================================================================
#
# **`N2` switches the user's device's 3.3 V supply.** It drives the LED of
# an AQV210 PhotoMOS relay through a series resistor, so a high here closes
# that relay and powers the device. It must be low at power-up and must go
# high only as the first step of the card half's activation sequence, which
# is the only thing in the design that can reach it:
#
#   - `vcc_en` comes out of `iso7816_terminal`'s sequencer and nothing else
#     drives it. `iso_display.v` passes it through; `iso_display_pad.v`
#     passes it to the ball.
#   - the register behind it initialises to zero, is cleared by the
#     power-on reset, and is set only by `A`.
#   - the display half cannot ask for it: no display command is an ISO
#     command, which is the rule the whole shared alphabet is built on.
#
# `J3`, `L3` and `M2` are the user's card's contact, clock and reset. Do not
# add a pin to this file that the design does not need; every one of these
# four goes somewhere that matters.
#
# **Pmod JXADC** for those four, chosen over JA/JB/JC because the board's
# owner confirmed visually that its anti-aliasing capacitors C33-C36 are
# **not populated**. That matters: a series resistor only costs low-level
# margin, but a shunt capacitor sits across an open-drain line and sets its
# rise time — even 1 nF with a 1k pull-up would make 2 Mbaud impossible.
# With the pads empty the rise is stray capacitance only, about 115 ns to
# 90 % at 50 pF, which is a fifth of a 500 ns etu.
#
#   JXADC1  J3   io         the card's single line, driven and read. Open
#                           drain: pulled low or released, never driven
#                           high, so the card and this board share it.
#   JXADC2  L3   clk_card   8.000 MHz, 112 MHz / 14, exactly 50 % duty
#   JXADC3  M2   rst_card   active low
#   JXADC4  N2   vcc_en     the AQV210 PhotoMOS relay's LED. See above.
#   JXADC5  —    GND
#
# **No `-pullup` on `io`**: the line already has an external 1k to 3.3 V.
# A second pull would change the timing the owner measured for.
#
# ===================================================================
# THE DISPLAY LINK
# ===================================================================
#
# The top row of **Pmod JA**, plus a ground. A Pmod connector is 12 pins,
# 2x6: pins 1-4 and 7-10 are the eight signals, 5 and 11 are ground, 6 and
# 12 are 3V3.
#
#   JA1   J1     sclk    the master's clock
#   JA2   L2     mosi    its data: commands and pixels on one wire
#   JA3   J2     dc      low for a command byte, high for a pixel byte
#   JA4   G2     cs_n    low while a byte is on the wire
#   JA5   —      GND     connect this: two boards need a common ground
#
# JA rather than JB because it is on the same edge as JXADC — shorter leads
# and one ground reference.
#
# **These four may be any ball of JA**, which was measured rather than
# assumed: all eight JA balls were built as plain inputs and every one
# decoded completely. The same balls used *bidirectionally* do not: L2, G2,
# K2 and G3 each put configuration bits in a tile this database has no
# segbits file for, so a bitstream using them as an inout cannot be fully
# decoded and checked. These four are inputs, so the gap does not reach
# them — but a design that made one of them bidirectional would be
# unverifiable, and that is worth knowing before anybody moves a wire.
#
# **All four are inputs.** This design is the display, not the driver.
#
# **3.3 V only.** The Basys 3's Pmod pins are 3.3 V CMOS. A 5 V master
# needs level shifting, and connecting one directly risks the part.
#
# **The rate limit is a ratio**: `PHASE_MARGIN = 2` in `spi_display_rx`
# means each `sclk` phase must last at least two system clocks, so at
# 112 MHz `sclk` may run up to 28 MHz — more margin than the 25 it had at
# 100 MHz, since the combined design clocks faster. An SSD1306's own
# maximum is well under either. `overrun_count` rises before any bit is
# lost, so the counters say when the margin has gone rather than dropping
# bits quietly.
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 clk W5

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 io       J3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 clk_card L3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 rst_card M2
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 vcc_en   N2

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 sclk J1
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 mosi L2
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 dc   J2
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 cs_n G2

# The far side's two button lines, on **Pmod JC** — reachable from the same
# side of the board as JA. These must be bidirectional: each is released
# except while pressed.
#
#   JC1  K17  btn_left   idles LOW  through a 10k pull-down -> press drives HIGH
#   JC2  M18  btn_right  idles HIGH through a 10k pull-up   -> press drives LOW
#
# No `-pullup`: the far side's own resistors define both idle levels, and a
# second pull would fight one of them.
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 btn_left  K17
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 btn_right M18

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 uart_rx_pin B18
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 uart_tx_pin A18

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[0]  U16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[1]  E19
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[2]  U19
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[3]  V19
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[4]  W18
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[5]  U15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[6]  V14
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[7]  V13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[8]  V3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[9]  W3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[10] U3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[11] P3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[12] N3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[13] P1
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[14] L1

# Segments a..g, the decimal point, and the four digit anodes.
# **`an[0]` is the RIGHT-hand digit**, CHECKED 8 October 2026 by reading
# `1f29` off the glass in `selftest.v` — a status byte then a sequence
# number in that order — so the most significant nibble is leftmost.
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[0] W7
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[1] W6
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[2] U8
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[3] V8
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[4] U5
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[5] V5
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[6] U7
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 dp     V7
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 an[0]  U2
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 an[1]  U4
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 an[2]  V4
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 an[3]  W4

create_clock -name sys -period 10.0 clk
clock_domain sys clk
